**Theorem 15.3 (same-graph repeated-service closure).** In the valid
late-installed version, every finite canonical-frame lawful cut has an
actual finite-completion-conditional later Cover, acquired immutable
reference and exact historical Call. This includes every cut inside
installation, every earlier Boundary/Prefix computation, bank or descriptor
construction, result publication, source overwrite, native ingress and
copy. Every prescribed non-IN successor stays in this domain; an IN
successor stays in it exactly when its bit preserves canonical-frame
extendibility, or is the prescribed fixed valid boot bit. The package's
old target never moves under later frames. No source-only replay assertion
or equality of original and extended clocks is used.

Proof. First prove termination of complete fragments by induction on the
number of completed frames after installation. The base is the actual
initialized Ready of Proposition 14.3, with Lemma 13.2a's exact imported
fields, finite entry graph and complete token/head successors. An original
source frame terminates on its same finite canonical request by LJA's
progress theorem and the explicit two-row
input adapter. It uses the actual source; its new retention and return
are finite word work. At the next service frame, all its cover backings
and package keys refer only to earlier completed prefixes. Their actual
native executions are finite by the induction hypothesis. Lemma 15.1
makes Boundary and Prefix finite on those histories; each simulated row
has finite word expansion, including rows inside earlier recoveries.
The other loops consume finite words and lists. Publish and return are
finite initialized copies and finite word work. This closes the induction.
There is no inference that a semantic self-call terminates merely because
its decoder was declared total.

For an unfinished canonical ingress, supply a finite canonical remainder;
then complete its already admitted finite fragment. At any other unfinished
phase, follow its unique prescribed finite work. A cut inside installation
is covered by completing that finite installation. The following Cover
has that exact old cut in its predecessor prefix. Lemma 15.1 and Next
or a genuinely acquired Select retrieve it. The all-input retention rule
covers added service choices, unlike the pair in Lemma 7.2. The row and
copy correspondence includes interior partial states, not only word or
service boundaries.

Canonical extendibility gives precisely the task's admitted IN edges,
as in AIF's domain theorem. Raw other bits still physically execute;
they leave this theorem's domain. Busy unauthorized requests have no
primitive successor. Every frozen package stores its actual manifest,
selected input prefix and fixed target word, so subsequent extension
changes none of its denotation. Source projection preserves each literal
TagRead and ReadE, whole guarded modifications and executed rejects;
new source frame clocks and origins are the new graph's actual ones.
Old pre-activation clocks are common because their prefix is identical;
a boot-installed variant would have a different entrance and is not
identified with these ordinals. ∎

